diff --git a/docs/data-transfers.md b/docs/data-transfers.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..17043cc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/data-transfers.md
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# Account and card data transfers
+
+Admins can request an account export in Account Settings. Members can request an
+export of their accessible cards from their own profile. Both run as background
+jobs and email a signed download link, valid for 24 hours from completion. Treat
+these links as secrets: anyone with the link can download the ZIP. Exports are
+purged by the hourly `Export.cleanup` task.
+
+A signed-in identity can upload an account ZIP at `/account/imports/new` without
+having an account on the destination installation. Imports create a new account;
+they never merge into or overwrite an existing account. The importing identity
+becomes its owner, with access to all its boards. Other people are matched by
+email address, or receive new identities. Existing identities are not modified.
+
+## Archive format (version 1)
+
+* `manifest.json`: format (`fizzy`), version, kind (`account` or `cards`), and
+  counts of entries by directory, used to detect incomplete archives.
+* `cards/<id>.json`: card attributes, HTML description, comments with their HTML
+  bodies, and attachment metadata. File metadata includes the blob ID, original
+  filename, content type and size. Binary content is at `files/<blob-id>`.
+* Account exports additionally contain `<table-name>/<id>.json` records from
+  `Account::DataTransfer::Manifest`, including users, boards, columns, comments,
+  rich text, blob metadata and attachment relationships. Saved-filter resources
+  are included in each filter's JSON.
+* Rich-text attachments and mentions use portable `fizzy:<type>:<id>` references.
+  Imports replace these with destination signed references.
+
+Personal card exports are not account backups and cannot be imported as accounts.
+Authentication credentials, sessions, access tokens, and other export archives
+are not part of an account backup. Imported public board keys and storage keys
+are regenerated. Imported webhooks are disabled and historical notification
+bundles are marked delivered to avoid sending old notifications or integrations.
+
+## Storage and failure handling
+
+ZIP64 output streams files in chunks. Disk exports use a tempfile; S3 exports use
+multipart streaming. Imports use range reads with read-ahead caching, and stream
+individual files to S3 (or use a per-file tempfile on disk). The entire archive is
+never loaded into application memory. ZIP directory reads are limited to 128 MiB
+and individual JSON entries to 32 MiB to bound untrusted input. Binary attachment
+sizes are not subject to the JSON limit. ZIP checksums, blob checksums, record
+references, and manifest counts are checked during import.
+
+The upload form uses multipart HTTP uploads. Configure proxy/request timeouts,
+body-size limits, and upload temporary-disk capacity for the largest archive you
+intend to accept, including 500+ GB archives. Background workers need sufficient
+runtime and, when using disk storage, room for archive/per-file temporary copies.
+
+All imported database records, including search and storage accounting, are built
+in one transaction. Failure rolls back that transaction and removes files uploaded
+by the import. The temporary input ZIP is deleted after processing. The importer
+receives an email with the new account link or a failure reason. Mail delivery is
+retried independently of the completed, idempotent data-transfer work.
